CloudBurrow patch 84 to github.com/goccy/bigquery-emulator v0.8.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1661). Needs 0080 and 0083, and googlesqlite
patch 0019.

The bug: every request finds its project (withProjectMiddleware), and
FindProject (0080) read and JSON-decoded the metadata of every table,
model and routine of every dataset of the project, while it held the
emulator's one request lock (sequentialAccessMiddleware). The CloudBurrow
front keeps each query's result as a table, so every request cost more
with every result kept: findDatasetsRaw took 8% of the emulator's CPU
with about 440 result tables and 17% with 610, and garbage collection a
fifth more.

The patch reads a dataset found by FindProject, FindAllProjects or
FindDataset with its own metadata alone. Its tables, models and routines
are read, as SQLite SQL keyed by the dataset and in the order they were
stored, when a handler first asks for every one of a kind (TableIDs,
Tables: tables.list, a dataset's deletion), and one of them by its
primary key when a handler asks for it by ID (Table, Model, Routine:
tables.get, a query's or a load's destination table, AddTable's check).
A read inside a transaction of the request goes through the connection
of that transaction (BindTx, 0083), the newest when the handler did not
name one: another connection to the shared-cache database could not read
a table the transaction has written. The datasets row's tableIDs,
modelIDs and routineIDs, which nothing reads, are written from the rows
the transaction sees when the dataset has not read them.
FindProjectWithConn, which has only a transaction, reads as before.

Dataset.DeleteModel held the dataset's lock while it wrote the dataset's
row, which reads its IDs under the same lock, and so waited for itself,
holding the request lock, forever; it releases the lock first.

The regression tests hold FindProject to no table, model or routine
decoded, and to the same allocations with 10 tables or 600.

